  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>McHugh Peak</strong> is a 4,298-foot summit in Alaska. The McHugh Peak Trail covers 11.8 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 2,913 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=61.03943,-149.5824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">61.03943, -149.5824</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>Late May through October is the main season. Snow and ice linger into spring and return early in fall at this latitude and elevation; in winter this becomes a different, more serious undertaking requiring traction devices and warm layers.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Black Mesa, Ptarmigan Peak and South Yuyanq\u2019 Ch\u2019ex \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
